\section{Method}

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation modality. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 97.4\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality, yielding no routing signal. We instead perform episode-level counterfactual analysis.

For each task instance, we execute two complete episodes under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation). The \emph{pure-internal} episode follows the ReAct paradigm \cite{react-2023}, where the LLM interleaves chain-of-thought reasoning with environment actions. The \emph{pure-deterministic} episode uses the environment simulator to compute exact state transitions for each action. In both cases the LLM selects which action to take; deterministic computation replaces only the state-transition prediction, not action selection---equivalent to giving the agent access to a perfect world model.

Each episode pair produces a tuple $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$, where $s$ denotes the terminal score and $d \in \{0,1\}$ indicates agent death. We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (vLLM) on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based environment spanning 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty, with episodes capped at 30 steps. After prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type).

\subsection{Death-Aware Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

Each counterfactual pair receives an episode-level routing label based on $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$. ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. The label is assigned by one of four mutually exclusive cases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Death asymmetry}: $d^{\text{int}} \neq d^{\text{det}}$ $\rightarrow$ the surviving strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Both dead}: $d^{\text{int}} = d^{\text{det}} = 1$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
    \item \textbf{Score comparison}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} \neq s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ the higher-scoring strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Tied alive}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} = s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
\end{enumerate}

Label distributions are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF):
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{nd}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is the mean score gap ($s^{\text{det}} - s^{\text{int}}$) across all episodes and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ excludes episodes where either strategy dies. DAF is well-defined when $\Delta_{\text{full}} > 0$; in our data, the minimum per-task $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $+9.58$ (\texttt{find-non-living-thing}), ensuring numerical stability. To assess sensitivity to penalty magnitude, we recalculate DAF under hypothetical death penalties (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}): $P{=}10 \rightarrow$ DAF $= 46.4\%$, $P{=}50 \rightarrow 81.2\%$, $P{=}100 \rightarrow 89.6\%$ (actual), $P{=}200 \rightarrow 94.5\%$. Death avoidance remains the dominant component across all penalty magnitudes, though its relative contribution scales with penalty severity.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter plot: death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). Pearson $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$). Each point is one of 11 ScienceWorld task types.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

The full performance gap between deterministic and internal strategies across 245 episodes is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. When we exclude all episodes where either strategy results in agent death, the gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$. The resulting Death Avoidance Factor (\S\ref{sec:daf}) is 90.41\%: approximately 90\% of the deterministic strategy's advantage over internal reasoning is attributable to death avoidance rather than superior task performance when both agents survive.

This concentration is consistent across task types (Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}). The Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$), as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}. Knowing how much more frequently the internal strategy dies is nearly sufficient to predict the full performance gap for a given task type. The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes compared to 16.3\% for deterministic computation---a 2.6$\times$ ratio that drives the bulk of the observed score difference.

The episode-level label distribution reflects this asymmetry: of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, only 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}---when internal reasoning does not cause death, it performs comparably to deterministic computation.

One task reveals the residual non-death signal. \texttt{find-non-living-thing} ($n=40$) has zero deaths under both strategies yet shows $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$, entirely from non-death performance differences---deterministic computation can provide advantages through more accurate state tracking even when agents survive. However, among the 10 death-prone task types, death avoidance overwhelms non-death differences. When both modalities survive, the gap is small but non-negligible ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$, 9.6\% of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 245 episodes). 95\% Bootstrap CIs. The oracle selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode; 96.3\% of its decisions match the always-deterministic strategy.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} reports episode-weighted mean scores and death rates for four unconditional strategies. Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points, consistent with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. The oracle, which selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode, improves only modestly to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of its routing decisions matching always-deterministic. This \emph{oracle degeneracy} indicates that the optimal episode-level routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation---the routing signal is insufficient to improve upon simply always choosing the deterministic strategy. Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies, with a death rate (27.8\%) proportional to its 50/50 allocation.

Keyword-based routing fails structurally: match rates cluster at extremes (85.8\% or $<$5\%), precluding meaningful routing discrimination.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

A natural question is whether the episode-level counterfactual labels can train a step-level router. We investigate this as a diagnostic probe of label informativeness rather than a routing system proposal, testing two architectures.

\paragraph{Classification head.} We append a linear classification head to the LLM's hidden representations, trained on episode-level routing labels. Across all configurations tested---class weighting, focal loss, minority undersampling, and temperature scaling---the classifier degenerates to predicting always-deterministic (0\% internal recall in all cases). The episode-level labeling granularity does not transfer to step-level classification through this architecture.

\paragraph{Implicit router.} We fine-tune Qwen3-1.7B to internalize routing decisions within its generation process (training details in Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}). At both the logit and generation levels, accuracy is 65.4\%, balanced accuracy 59.3\%, and internal recall 51.5\%---above chance but insufficient for practical routing. Cross-seed balanced accuracy is stable (57--59\%), though internal recall varies widely (51.5--81.8\%; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}).

\paragraph{End-to-end evaluation.} Deployed as an episode-level router (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}), the trained router scores $-10.70$ (95\% CI $[-16.33, -5.27]$), not significantly outperforming always-deterministic ($-9.47$, $[-14.71, -4.29]$). Logit-level success rules out uninformative labels, pointing to the episode-to-step granularity mismatch as the bottleneck. When DAF $\approx 90\%$, router headroom is structurally limited to the ${\sim}10\%$ non-death signal, and any router that occasionally routes internally risks catastrophic failure.

\paragraph{Data split.} Of the 245 collected episodes, 95 receive a non-tie label (det-preferred or internal-preferred) and are used for router training. We apply episode-stratified 85/15 train/validation split (81 train, 14 validation episodes; 1734 train, 306 validation steps). Table~\ref{tab:baselines} baselines (always-internal, always-deterministic, random) report descriptive statistics of collected trajectory scores, while the trained router score ($-10.70$) comes from fresh environment interaction (end-to-end evaluation on new episodes), not evaluation on training data.

\paragraph{Labeling quality.} We verify label quality in Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}.

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool-Use Learning.}
Toolformer \cite{toolformer-2024} inserts API calls via self-supervised filtering; ToolkenGPT \cite{toolkengpt-2023} represents tools as embeddings in the token space; ART \cite{art-2023} and Chameleon \cite{chameleon-2024} compose tools under LLM orchestration; ReTool \cite{retool-2026} applies RL for code-interpreter invocation.
We differ by generating routing labels via \emph{episode-level counterfactual analysis}, framing routing as \emph{catastrophic failure prevention}, and training an explicit lightweight router for diagnostic analysis.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation.}
CogRouter \cite{cogrouter-2026} routes among cognitive depth levels on ScienceWorld; ARES \cite{ares-2026} selects per-step reasoning effort; Mixture-of-Recursions \cite{mixture-of-recursions-2025} applies adaptive depth at the architecture level.
MTRouter \cite{mtrouter-2026} routes among LLMs to optimize cost-quality tradeoffs.
These methods route \emph{how much} or \emph{which model}; we route \emph{which modality} and find that death avoidance accounts for ${\sim}90\%$ of the routing signal.

\paragraph{World Model Routing.}
ITP \cite{itp-2026} and Dyna-Think \cite{dynathink-2025} route to approximate LLM-based world models that inherit hallucination failures.
Our deterministic modality provides exact state transitions, and our analysis reveals that this exactness matters almost exclusively for death avoidance.

\paragraph{Agent Frameworks.}
ReAct \cite{react-2023} interleaves reasoning and acting and serves as our internal-reasoning baseline. ALFWorld \cite{alfworld-2021} provides a text-based household benchmark complementary to ScienceWorld; Voyager \cite{voyager-2023} demonstrates open-ended LLM agents with learned skill libraries.
We analyze \emph{when} reasoning should yield to deterministic computation, finding the answer concentrated in catastrophic failure scenarios. Our finding connects to the safe RL literature, where avoiding catastrophic states is formalized as a hard constraint \citep{garcia2015comprehensive}.

\paragraph{Concurrent Work.}
Concurrent studies examine complementary aspects: cognitive illusions driving tool overuse \cite{tool-overuse-illusion-2026}, hidden-state encoding of tool-call decisions \cite{when2tool-2026}, a ``tool-use tax'' where tools are not always beneficial \cite{tool-use-tax-2026}, and necessity/utility/affordability evaluation \cite{to-call-or-not-2026}. Our death avoidance decomposition provides concrete quantification---${\sim}90\%$ of routing signal stems from catastrophic-failure prevention---grounding these frameworks in interactive-environment evidence.

